WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.760
Congo Holdup is the biggest leak of data

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.283
in the history of Africa.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.620
And more than 3.5 million documents

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.470
of financial data that comes
from a bank called BGFI

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.660
show the world what's been happening

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.240
in Congo at this one
particular bank in a way

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.200
and at a level of detail
that we've never seen before.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.050
The sort of overarching
discovery was to show

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.280
how the most powerful
political family in the country

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.870
and its friends had sort
of tooled up this bank

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:42.290
to transfer large amounts of
state funds to themselves.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.810
And what we found out in the leak

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.310
had a lot of very interesting
things to say about

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.470
the way some of the
Chinese mining companies

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.270
that have come to dominate
this sector in recent years

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.360
have operated in the
country, and in particular,

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.560
the relationship between
them and the entourage

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.963
around former president Joseph Kabila.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.210
Laurent Kabila was laid to rest a week

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.950
after being shot by his own bodyguard

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:17.240
in one of the most bizarre
of political assassinations.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.800
He's succeeded by his son Joseph,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.750
believed by many to be under ...

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.780
Joseph Kabila was the President of

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.660
the Democratic Republic of
Congo for almost two decades.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.210
He came into power in
2001 as a very young man.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.850
He was only 29.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.000
He succeeded his father
who had also been president

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.140
who was assassinated
by a trusted bodyguard.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.040
Kabila senior had come to
power only a couple of years

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.810
earlier in 1997 by marching a rebel army

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.100
right across the vastness of Congo

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.690
and overthrowing a dictator
who had been in power

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.253
for more than 30 years.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.290
But soon after Laurent
Kabila overthrew Mobutu,

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.920
he kind of continued the
kleptocratic ways of Mobutu,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.820
and his former patrons
Rwanda and Uganda got angry

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.690
and they started another war.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.580
And this became what's
known as Africa's world war

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.010
and millions of people died.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.860
Almost all of Congo's
neighbors became involved.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.900
There is no security.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.323
People are plundering.

00:02:23.395 --> 00:02:26.940
They are doing what they call pillage.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.773
It was bloody.

00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:29.390
It was long.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.550
And it ended up dividing the
country into several slices

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.670
with different rebel groups
controlling different parts

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.250
and different countries allied

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.130
to those different rebel groups.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.560
If we are able to reunify Congo,

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.390
we will be able to do anything else.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.400
Joseph Kabila actually
brought that war to an end

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.150
in 2003, negotiating a settlement with

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.403
all the various countries
that were involved.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.940
Soon he was eventually
elected president in 2006

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.160
and he took over a country that is one of

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.050
the richest countries in the world

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.300
in terms of natural resources,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.383
but its population is one of the poorest.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.270
Joseph Kabila was looking
to rebuild the country,

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.570
rebuild the economy and he needed cash.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:25.550
He tried to open up Congo's
vast and world-class reserves

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:30.240
of copper, cobalt, gold to
international investors.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.010
At the beginning, there were
plenty of western companies

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.430
who showed interests in investing in

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.270
and developing these assets.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:41.690
But over time their interest has dwindled.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.420
And they've departed partly
because of the unpredictability

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.540
of Congolese politics,

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.187
partly because of the reputation
for corruption in Congo.

00:03:51.187 --> 00:03:52.610
But this isn't to say that

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.540
there are not major
western companies there.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.050
In fact, the biggest producer of cobalt,

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:02.750
it may well be the biggest
producer of copper is Glencore

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.923
which is headquartered in Switzerland.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:09.520
But however, in recent years,
the trajectory has been

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.173
more and more Chinese companies coming in.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.590
Congo has a very, very
significant mining industry.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.270
It's Africa's biggest producer of copper.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:23.840
It's by far the world's
biggest producer of cobalt.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.850
And say the last 10 to 15
years Chinese companies

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.290
making the running, piling
in with investments.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.290
So they've gone from a
pretty bit part player

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.883
to the largest producers
of both metals in Congo.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.350
The deal that really
turbocharged the involvement

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.160
of Chinese mining companies
in the Congolese mining sector

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.593
happened in 2008.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.090
And according to this deal,
Chinese companies would build

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.640
three billion dollars of infrastructure

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.440
and also invest in additional $3.2 billion

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.803
in a huge copper, cobalt mining
project called Sicomines.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:16.990
And it would be profits
generated by sales of copper

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.060
and cobalt from Sicomines

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.370
that would pay back both
investments in the infrastructure

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.573
and in the mine itself.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:29.780
Sicomines is the mining component of

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.660
what was known at one point
as the deal of the century,

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.660
basically trading Congo's
minerals, copper and cobalt

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.910
for three billion dollars worth
of infrastructure projects

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.450
because Congo is in desperate
need after years of war

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.400
for roads, for bridges,
for schools and hospitals.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.200
The Chinese started to provide that.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.010
The Sicomines mine, or
more accurately, mines

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:57.090
are located just outside Kolwezi

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:01.240
which is a major mining
hub in southeastern Congo.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.260
And I've visited the mine several times.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.580
It is world-class, massive.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:09.420
You see trucks that are
the size of a house,

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.170
sometimes hundreds of trucks,
just in a row of procession

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:15.200
coming up and down the
switchbacks of the mine

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.640
to get the ore out of the mine,

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.960
and then it goes to a
massive processing plant,

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:24.320
again, world-class, that produces
copper cathodes and cobalt

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.230
which then gets shipped
to China to be turned into

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.290
pipes and wiring and car
batteries and all the things

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.403
that are so important
for the green revolution.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:39.880
There has always been a
quite considerable lack

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.420
of transparency around
the Sicomines project.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.410
And also the infrastructure projects

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.490
that Sicomines is paying for

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:53.830
because Sicomines' profits
are more or less tax free

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.890
until the debts are all paid off.

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:01.210
There is a lot of irritation
and concern within the Congo

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.590
that this huge mining
project is not really

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.280
producing any benefit for Congo

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.823
in terms of tax revenue
and royalty revenue.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.990
So the leak was obtained
by a French anti-corruption

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.950
organization called The Platform

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:35.330
to Protect Whistleblowers in Africa

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.640
and the French investigative
news website MediaPart.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.040
And then it was shared with
Bloomberg and with a number

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.730
of other media outlets and
NGOs throughout the world

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:48.610
to comb through these documents
over many, many months.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.980
We can't disclose the
source of these documents

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.650
because it's just not safe.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:55.310
In the leak, there were
all kinds of threads

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.840
that we could pull.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:00.700
One was that the bank at
the heart of this project,

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:04.180
which is called BGFI.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.370
It's a Gabonese bank and
it's a pan-African bank

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.420
that has branches all over Africa

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.530
but also in France as well.

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:15.390
It often partners with local elites

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:16.840
in the countries where it operates.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.350
And in the case of Congo,
a sister of the president

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:22.370
who took a minority stake in the bank.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.630
The amazing thing is that the sister

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.450
of the president actually
never paid for her

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:27.943
40% stake in the bank.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.147
And one of Kabila's
brothers, Francis Selemani

00:08:32.147 --> 00:08:35.480
became the bank's chief executive.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.570
So right from the
beginning of the existence

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:43.570
of BGFI Bank's unit in Congo,
it isn't really a stretch

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:47.173
to call it a Kabila family company.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.690
Basically over a six-year
period, this bank had acted

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:56.690
as a conduit to funnel at
least 138 million dollars

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.105
in public funds to companies
controlled by the family

00:09:01.105 --> 00:09:06.105
of the then president Joseph
Kabila or associates of him.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.100
That money came from the
Congolese Central Bank.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:14.250
It came from Congo's
state-owned mining company

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.970
and it even came from the
agency responsible for

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:21.383
the upkeep of Congo's dire road network.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.350
The other thing it allowed
us to do for the first time

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:29.070
was show how major Chinese
corporations were giving money

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:32.190
to companies that eventually
transferred that money

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:34.340
to businesses that were
linked to Kabila family

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.453
and associates.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:41.050
One of the key figures of
interest in our research

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:44.670
was a Chinese businessman named Du Wei

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.170
and his company, Congo
Construction Company

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:53.170
which held two accounts in
BGFI Bank's Congolese unit.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:56.510
What's amazing about Congo
Construction Company is that

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:58.950
we don't think it's constructed anything.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.930
We've found nothing that they've built

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:05.010
over almost a decade
of existence in Congo.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.590
And yet we found tens
of millions of dollars

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.770
flowing through their
bank accounts from major

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:15.180
established Chinese companies
through the BGFI Bank

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.163
to businesses linked to the Kabila family.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.980
And Du Wei was born in
1979, but he was in Congo

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:24.690
in his early twenties, already

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.970
doing deals and doing projects.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.850
And he actually worked for Sicomines,

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.940
the mine at the heart of
the China-Congo contract.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.020
And then amazingly, he switched sides.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.410
He switched to the Congolese side

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.550
and started working with the office

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.190
that managed the China-Congo contract.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:43.440
And soon after that, he created a company

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.463
with a Congolese lawyer,
Congo Construction Company.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.580
He was also a PhD student
at the University of Wuhan.

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:55.260
He published a paper which
was complaining about

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.650
the tendency of Chinese
companies to engage in

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.170
what he called unscrupulous means

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:03.110
such as bribery and corruption

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:05.590
to win major infrastructure projects

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:07.233
in the developing world.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:17.560
So probably the most sort of
brazen and illuminating way

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:21.210
in which Du Wei and Congo
Construction Company's role

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:25.557
as an intermediary was
demonstrated was in 2016

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:31.060
when Sicomines transferred
25 million dollars

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:33.780
to Congo Construction Company.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.470
Du Wei subsequently
distributed most of this money

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:40.900
among people associated
with Joseph Kabila.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.440
7.5 million dollars went to a company

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.385
controlled by Kabila's sister
and Kabila's sister-in-law.

00:11:48.385 --> 00:11:53.385
1.6 million was wired to a
company in the Faroe Islands

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:57.970
that operated a vessel which was used

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:02.936
to transport animals, such as wildebeests,

00:12:02.936 --> 00:12:04.811
zebras, giraffes,

00:12:04.811 --> 00:12:08.380
to Joseph Kabila's private nature reserve

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:10.190
just outside Kinshasa.

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.850
And another million dollars was sent to

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:17.373
the director of this company.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.030
Some of these very large
transactions are questionable

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.200
simply because they don't seem to have

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:27.810
a legitimate commercial
justification behind them.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.190
And Joseph Kabila is the very, very guy

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:34.420
who gave Sicomines the right to develop

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.563
these very, very lucrative
copper and cobalt permits.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:40.930
The money that was supposed
to fund the infrastructure

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:45.460
for Congolese citizens, you
know, took away, you know

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.200
The money gone to president Kabila.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:48.760
So it was so surprising.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:50.990
Because you see the agreement is like,

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:52.630
okay, we are going to
fund the infrastructure.

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.740
And definitely understand
why we didn't see

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.558
infrastructure in the Congo.

00:12:56.558 --> 00:12:58.693
I think it's really, this is really crazy.

00:13:01.465 --> 00:13:06.465
Thank you for the
opportunity and the honor

00:13:06.717 --> 00:13:11.717
of having shared your
experience and your wisdom

00:13:13.033 --> 00:13:15.616
with me over the last 17 years.

00:13:17.564 --> 00:13:21.720
Basically since 2018, the year
before Kabila stepped down

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:25.070
which was also the year
where BGFI Bank forced out

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.650
his brother and his sister from the bank

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:31.740
amid accumulating scandals
caused by a smaller leak

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:33.690
of information the year before.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:37.700
Ever since then, BGFI Bank
has been trying to draw a line

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:41.993
under its association
with the Kabila family.

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:47.440
BGFI Bank declined to respond
to any of our questions

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.780
before we began publishing the
results of our investigation.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:55.310
They eventually did put out
a statement on their website.

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:59.540
BGFI said that there had
been management issues

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:01.760
at the Congo branch of BGFI

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:04.350
and that they'd addressed them years ago.

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:06.620
It did, however, also question

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.520
why these documents were
leaked and suggested that

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.910
maybe those documents, some
were forged or inauthentic,

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.770
and they suggested that they would pursue

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.173
anyone who leaked these documents.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:23.470
Joseph Kabila never responded
to multiple requests

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:25.150
from the Congo Hold-up Consortium,

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.160
or Bloomberg News about the allegations

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:30.160
that were made through our investigations.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.620
Some of his associates did come out

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:34.350
and sullied the investigation,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:35.810
suggested that they were inauthentic,

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:39.610
suggested that there were
some kind of smear campaign

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:41.313
attacking the former president.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:58.920
Du Wei never responded to any
of Congo Hold-up's questions

00:14:58.920 --> 00:14:59.980
or questions from Bloomberg.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:03.150
And in fact, he seems to have
deleted his WhatsApp account

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.770
after we've bombarded
him with questions about

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:07.240
his business dealings in Congo.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:08.613
He's no longer in Congo.

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:12.520
Sicomines denied all allegations.

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:16.510
They said that they were
fictitious and made up

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:18.700
and that they'd always
worked within the bounds

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:20.570
of Congolese law and international law

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.703
when it comes to good business practices.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.350
I think one of the most
interesting takeaways

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:30.670
is the scale.

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:32.560
We're talking about one bank,

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:35.350
a relatively modest Congolese bank

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:39.330
over a six or seven-year period,

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:41.920
and this is only the money that we can be

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.210
absolutely sure about that we can trace

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.893
to the satisfaction of
our reporting standards.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.570
It's really a lot of money,
especially when you consider

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:52.620
that this is happening in
one of the poorest countries

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:53.453
on the planet.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:56.830
One of the things that Congo
Hold-up tried to highlight

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:59.950
is that there are different actors.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:03.320
They put in place a complex
and complicated system

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:05.250
with the purpose to steal money.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.610
Without Congo Hold-up investigation,

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.470
I don't think it was even possible for us

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.913
to definitely understand, you
know, who played which role?

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:18.347
It's not a surprise that
there is corruption in Congo.

00:16:18.347 --> 00:16:21.440
The Congolese know this
better than anyone.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:23.620
They experience it on a daily basis.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.780
The difference with the
Congo Hold-up consortium is

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.540
that we were able to bring the receipts.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:31.550
That has been a boon for
Congolese civil society

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:34.810
and so there are a lot of
groups, Congolese groups

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:39.060
that are now fighting
to hold them accountable

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.960
and so that's an exciting development.

